Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA) Equivalent
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Sudden focal neurologic deficit (e.g., amaurosis fugax) | Complete symptomatic resolution <24 hours | Absence of acute infarction on DWI MRI sequence

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-030
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe distress following a brief episode of neurological dysfunction, requiring urgent evaluation to exclude an impending major ischemic stroke or critical carotid stenosis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate a sudden, transient focal neurological deficit. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out an evolving cerebrovascular accident or unstable plaque, preventing a catastrophic major stroke prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: For about ten minutes, my whole right arm went completely dead and heavy, and my speech sounded like I was drunk, but now it is totally fine.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a sudden onset of a focal neurological deficit occurring approximately two hours ago. The transient duration resolved completely within twenty minutes. Symptoms included unilateral weakness and heaviness in the right upper limb, accompanied by sudden isolated speech difficulty (dysarthria). The patient denies amaurosis fugax (transient curtain over vision). There was pronounced unilateral tingling in the right hand. The patient explicitly denies migrainous features such as a slowly spreading sensory aura or severe headache.
Functional Status: Fully independent; currently functioning normally at baseline.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe anxiety regarding imminent stroke risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 158/94 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm (Regular) | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Patient appears alert, oriented, and highly anxious, but in no current physical distress. A review of routine labs from earlier this week incidentally notes an asymptomatic bacteriuria finding (incidental positive culture, complete absence of dysuria, >10^5 CFU/mL isolated), which is unrelated to the acute neurological event.
Physical Exam: Comprehensive formal neurological examination reveals a complete absence of residual clinical signs. Cranial nerves II-XII are intact. Motor strength is perfectly symmetric (5/5 bilaterally). Sensory examination to light touch and pinprick is completely normal. Cerebellar testing is flawless. Speech is perfectly articulate and fluent. Cardiovascular examination reveals a regular rhythm (no atrial fibrillation) and no audible carotid bruits.
Environmental Observations: Safe home environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient is not currently prescribed any antiplatelet agents or anticoagulants.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA) Equivalent - Status: High-risk resolved episode, impending stroke risk remains high.
Differentials:

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out clinically strictly due to the complete and rapid resolution of all focal neurological deficits upon physical examination.

Hemiplegic Migraine - Ruled out due to the abrupt (non-spreading) onset of symptoms, absence of headache, and lack of personal or family history of migraine.

Seizure (Todd's Paralysis) - Ruled out due to the absence of positive motor symptoms (jerking), preserved consciousness during the event, and rapid complete recovery.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Anxiety).

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

This patient required immediate, high-priority after-hours assessment following a transient focal neurological deficit. Telephone triage correctly identified the extreme risk of an impending major ischemic stroke within the subsequent 24 to 48 hours. Thorough clinical examination confirmed the complete resolution of the deficit, differentiating the event from a completed stroke but firmly establishing the diagnosis of a high-risk TIA. By conducting this rigorous assessment, the clinician was able to immediately initiate secondary stroke prevention pharmacology in the home, rapidly securing the patient's cerebrovascular status and orchestrating a highly coordinated, urgent outpatient TIA clinic referral rather than defaulting to an unindicated, late-night emergency department bed wait.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediately administered a loading dose of oral antiplatelet therapy (Aspirin 300mg) following negative contraindication screening. The expected investigation results include normal diffusion-weighted magnetic resonance imaging of the brain without acute restricted diffusion, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis via the rapid access TIA clinic.
Positive Behavioral Change: To immediately reduce cerebrovascular stress, engage in deep, slow diaphragmatic breathing for five minutes to lower your acute panic and sympathetic drive; actively managing your stress response will directly lower your currently elevated blood pressure and reduce sheer stress on your blood vessels.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Urgent referral generated for the Rapid Access TIA Clinic within 24 hours for carotid Doppler and formal neuroimaging.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops any recurrent weakness, facial droop, slurred speech, visual loss, or sudden severe loss of balance.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; TIA is a clinical diagnosis that represents a severe warning sign for a major future stroke.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves an extremely high risk of a recurrent, potentially permanent stroke within the next 48 hours without strict compliance to new medical therapies and rapid specialist review.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention via ambulance (call 000) if any of the symptoms return, even if they are milder, or if you suddenly lose your vision in one eye.

